From 03e98541855b7bd71e3df3e0043e5ffa83b79dac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "gastown.slit (polecat)" <polecat-gastown.slit@soundit.local>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 23:32:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(billing): add weekly, bimonthly, semiannual billing
 frequencies (psa-aci)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

GitHub #103. BillingPeriod only offered Monthly/Quarterly/Annually; MSPs
need weekly, every-two-months, and twice-a-year cadences too.

Adds three enum cases (Weekly, Bimonthly, Semiannual) in ascending-cadence
order so the billing-period dropdowns read shortest-to-longest. Because the
profile/contract forms already iterate BillingPeriod::cases() and validation
already uses `new Enum(BillingPeriod::class)`, the new options and their
acceptance flow through automatically. The billing_period column is a
string(20), so no migration is needed.

Weekly is the wrinkle: it is not a whole number of months, so the old
`months(): int` helper could not represent it, and a 0 there would
divide-by-zero the dashboard MRR rollup and loop forever in the profile
"cycles behind" catch-up math. Replaced `months()` with two intent-revealing
methods and routed all three call sites through them:
  - advance(CarbonInterface): CarbonInterface — one billing cycle forward.
    Weekly is a 7-day step; the month-based cadences keep the prior
    addMonths() overflow semantics. Used by BillingService::advanceNextRunDate
    and the profiles/show "cycles behind" loop.
  - monthsPerCycle(): float — months spanned by a cycle, for MRR
    normalisation (Weekly = 12/52 ≈ 0.23, never 0). Used by DashboardController.

Validation: tests/Unit/Enums/BillingPeriodTest (18 cases / 372 assertions)
covers labels, per-cadence advance dates, input non-mutation, monthsPerCycle
values, the positive-divisor guard, weekly MRR ($10/wk => $43.33/mo), and an
end-to-end catch-up loop that proves weekly advance terminates. Pint clean on
changed files. Full generateInvoice() E2E is not exercised — nextInvoiceNumber()
is MySQL-only on the sqlite test DB (pre-existing psa-sxhu) — but advance() is
a pure one-liner delegate, fully covered by the enum unit test.
